"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Favorable findings include negative surgical margins (0.0), no seminal vesicle invasion (0.0), and absence of lymphovascular invasion (0.0). The preoperative PSA was elevated at 11. The surgical pathology showed a Gleason 4+3 (ISUP grade group 4) and pathological T2 stage, which is a less favorable finding compared to the biopsy grade of 3+3 (ISUP grade group 1). The absence of lymph node removal means nodal status is unknown, which widens uncertainty.\n\nCountervailing factors include the negative margins and lack of extension. The difference between the biopsy grade and the prostatectomy grade is a point of divergence. The lack of documented postoperative PSA or follow-up significantly limits the ability to assess the subsequent trajectory of the disease. Missing nodal sampling further complicates the assessment of local control. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its subsequent trend would be crucial in revising this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 51.41 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 33.42–69.40 months; its coverage has not been validated."
